  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern is likely into early next\nweek. Light freezing rain is expected this morning over the \nnorth bringing travel impacts post holiday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure currently sits over SW PA and will likely head\nnortheast toward area of greatest pressure falls this morning.\nOverrunning pcpn is already falling and has made it as far north\nas Lincoln per latest webcam. Movement further to the north is \nbeing affected by dry air per 00z KCAR raob and dwpt depressions\nof 5-10 degrees as of 06z. KCAR raob has a warm layer of +5C\naround 800mb, however with extremely dry air in place cannot\nrule out brief snow at onset of pcpn as atmosphere dynamically\ncools. Bangor has reported -FZRASN as temps have remained at\n32F. Cannot rule out slippery roadways around the Bangor area\nvery early this morning with locations in the vicinity hovering\naround freezing. \n\nPcpn will spread north moistening the airmass and bringing\nmainly freezing rain to central sections in the next hour and\nfurther to the north after 5am. With temps having dropped lower\nthan anticipated expect frzg rain to continue across the north\ninto early this afternoon. All told we are still expecting a\nglaze of ice in the Bangor Region over the next hour or two with\na few hundredths to one tenth of an inch of ice mainly north \nand west of the I95 corridor. \n\nThe most northern areas have a rough end time of 1pm or later \nwith still the possibility of localized freezing drizzle due to \nstruggling surface temperatures.\n\nWrap around snow showers are expected over the northwest tonight\ninto Saturday morning with rain showers expected over the \nremainder of nrn areas. Rain and snow accumulations are expected\nto be light.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous marine conditions and possible coastal \nimpacts around the time of the midday high tide today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh tide around 17z today continues to pose a threat for minor\nsplash-over along coastal roads. Maximum surge values will be a\nfew hours before low tide this morning with a surge of 0.5 to \n0.8 foot at today/s midday high tide. With seas of 6-12 feet \nand wave period of > 10s cannot rule out splashover along the \nroads closest to the shore.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another low pressure system is expected to\nimpact the area Monday, though track and impacts remain\nuncertain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nOn Sunday, a large trough will be located over the Ohio Valley \nand will swing to the northeast. The developing cyclone will \nstrengthen as it approaches New England. The exact track of the \nlow center is not certain, but models are in relatively good \nagreement it will be somewhere over Maine on Monday. Strong \nwinds will be the primary concern with this system, but the low \nlocation will determine how strong the winds will be. The latest\nforecast has the low just inland from the coast, which will \nbring higher winds out of the southeast right along the coast. \nIf the low is further north, winds will be weaker, and if the \nlow is more off shore, winds will also be weaker and less \nonshore. If the low is just inland, the southeast winds could \nproduce some coastal flooding concerns. Precipitation will \nmainly be in the form of rain, with some snow mixing in across \nthe north. The timing and track of the low will become clearer \nas we get closer to the event.",
